From 7a3812385608e00c1fbd293a0ae70b22ca4f93e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 14:30:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): origin applies a worker's kanban_move of a
 worker-first card as a Board repin; back off on not_claimable [daveey]

Seen 2026-11-03 on t_01a1a13d757b23af (hub, worker-first-v1): worker-1 claimed it, the run moved it to
machine:hub (kanban_move) twice, and the Board reserved it for worker-1 again each time, followed by
"409 not_claimable", until the card self-blocked asking David to fix placement.

Why 1: the hub reserved it for worker-1 because, on the hub and on the Board, the card was still a
worker-first policy card with no machine:hub pin (the hub row reads runs_on NULL; reserved events carry
lifecycle.runs_on null).
Why 2: kanban_move (move_task, the running-card path) wrote runs_on locally on worker-1 and reported ok,
but a policy card's runs_on is Board-owned: acknowledged_outbox strips it from the push and the Board
fences the edit. Only move_queued routed policy cards to the Board repin; move_task did not.
Why 3: the Board repin is creator-only (the origin), and nothing on the origin read the worker's move.
Why the 409 repeated: reevaluate re-asked reserve for a card the reserved worker had just leased, each
dispatcher tick, at the same stale revision; nothing remembered the refusal.
Not caught: test_kanban_move only moves cards without a placement policy, and the repin tests cover only
move_queued from the origin; no test ran a policy card's move from a pool worker's run.

Fix:
- move_task on a policy card no longer writes the stripped runs_on; its moved event carries
  repin_requested and the result says the origin applies it (repin: requested).
- reevaluate (origin placement tick): a policy card whose latest moved event is an unapplied request is
  repinned with the Board's creator CAS repin to the requested labels (policy cleared, pin set) instead
  of being reserved; the applied repin is recorded as a moved event flagged repinned, so it never replays.
  The origin's dispatcher then claims a machine:<origin> card under its own Board lease.
- a 409 not_claimable from reserve/repin backs off for that card at that revision (600 s, or until the
  revision changes) instead of re-asking every tick. Other refusals keep today's retry.

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_worker_move_request.py (7). RED on base: the worker->hub move e2e
(worker run moves, origin repins, hub dispatcher leases it, worker-1 cannot), the repin-instead-of-reserve
unit, and the not_claimable backoff (3 reserves -> 1).
